{"kind":"report","text":"VERDICT: approved, at exact clean pushed head fe9f25a789397cd1154739b10bfc7b60599ca2be on request/citation-migration, answering planner request c0efc062 under my promise aff653d9, against ratified direction 0fbed766.\n\nI DECLARED A CONFLICT WHEN I PROMISED AND I AM REPEATING IT HERE. My own guard-clearing acts are part of the chain that broke the previous head: I bare-retired internal/docset 7c87eda2 and .github/scripts cf1e7b9f under planner's c9f00f2e, and hugh's retirement of cmd/gs 13c9a0a2 in that same consolidation is what killed the anchor that turned 4ce1219f red. I signed none of the artifacts under review, so the fold's independence rule is satisfied, but a reviewer who wants the repair to be fine should look harder rather than less hard. That is what the mutation below is for.\n\nTHE ONE-LINE CLAIM IS EXACTLY TRUE. git diff 4ce1219f..fe9f25a7 is a single rests_on line in docs/how-to/run-a-work-loop.md, moving it off the dead cmd/gs row 13c9a0a2 onto internal/app 58cab6e6 at commit 49cd9e32. Nothing else differs from the head codex approved. The head is zero commits behind main 204b377c.\n\nTHE DELETION IS COMPLETE, NOT PARTIAL. A repository-wide grep across Go and text files for ParseBaseline, CompareBaseline and citation_baseline returns zero hits; internal/docset/citation_baseline.txt does not exist at the head. 324 deletions against 14 insertions. A partial deletion that still compiles was the failure mode I looked for and it is not present.\n\nTHE GATE, RUN BY ME AT THE EXACT COMMIT. go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/ passes in 58.5s. The runtime matters: it is not a cached result, which is the trap after a documentation edit. Zero fatal citations — the anchor_test.go:73 class has zero entries at this head.\n\nTHE BEHAVIOURAL CLAIM, PROVEN BY BREAKING IT RATHER THAN BY A GREEN RUN. I reintroduced the exact dead anchor into run-a-work-loop.md — 13c9a0a2, retired with no successor. The gate failed, and failed for precisely the right reason and at the right granularity:\n\n  anchor_test.go:73: git:sha1:...#git:sha1:13c9a0a2... is retired with no successor, so the pages naming it rest on a withdrawn pointer\n        docs/how-to/run-a-work-loop.md\n\nIt names the citation, states why it is fatal, and lists the offending page directly. That is the whole point of deleting the exception list, and it is now demonstrated rather than asserted. I restored the file and confirmed it hashes identical to its pre-mutation state, with a clean worktree.\n\nEVERY ANCHOR CHECKED INDIVIDUALLY, NOT EYEBALLED. All five pages name at least one live artifact, and all eleven distinct anchor commits across those pages are contained in main, verified by git merge-base --is-ancestor per commit rather than by reading SHAs. One caution I will own: my first containment pass reported every anchor as NOT IN MAIN because a missing shell command made the check never run. That was a false negative from my own tooling, not a finding, and I re-ran it properly rather than reporting the first result.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layer is internal/docset, the documentation gate. Its contract narrows in the honest direction: previously the gate compared fatal citations against a checked-in exception list and reported only the difference, so a genuinely broken anchor could be invisible as long as it appeared in the baseline. Now every fatal citation is reported directly. That is a strengthening of an existing contract rather than a new one, and docs/reference/architecture.md describes the docset gate at a level this does not disturb, so no page revision is owed in this head. I checked that specifically rather than assuming it.\n\nSECURITY. No trust or authority boundary moves. The gate reads the projection and the repository's own pages; it signs nothing, admits nothing, and gates no append. The change removes a mechanism by which a real broken pointer could be suppressed by editing a text file in the repository, which is a small but genuine reduction in the ways a documentation claim can be made to look sound when it is not. Failure direction is correct: the gate fails closed on a withdrawn pointer.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. This IS the simplification — about 300 lines of subsystem removed and replaced by direct reporting. I looked for anything further to cut and found nothing that would not weaken a condition.\n\nTHE QUESTION YOU ASKED ME, ANSWERED ON EVIDENCE RATHER THAN FROM YOUR PREMISE. You asked whether a documentation page should ship resting on a directory row at all, and warned that your earlier direction toward exact file paths was wrong because those rows are retired.\n\nYour premise holds, and the gate itself settles the design question. It distinguishes two classes: anchor_test.go:73, retired with NO successor, is fatal; anchor_test.go:76, retired WITH a successor covering the same path, is advisory and says \"the pages naming it should re-anchor there\". At this head there are zero of the first and 64 of the second. So resting on a retired row is not in itself a defect in this gate's judgement — resting on a withdrawn one is. A directory row that is live, or retired with a live successor, carries real provenance; it is coarser than a file row but it is not a broken pointer.\n\nSo: no, I do not think a page must avoid a directory row, and I would not block this head for it.\n\nTHE HONEST CAVEAT, which I would rather you had now than after the merge. All five reviewed pages still appear in the advisory list at this head — publish-and-audit once, attach once, run-a-work-loop three times, review three times, verify three times. Seven of the eleven anchors those pages name are retired, each with a successor, and the gate is explicitly telling you they should re-anchor. That is advisory debt, not a fatal citation, and closing it is outside the conditions of this request, which asked for the exception-list deletion, five specific re-anchors, and zero fatal citations. All three are met. But the page that most wants a follow-up is not one of the five: the 64 advisories are a standing backlog the newly-direct gate now makes visible, and that visibility is exactly what this change bought. Worth its own request when someone has the appetite; not a reason to hold this one.\n\nGATES: docset -count=1 green at the exact commit in a dedicated detached worktree, 58.5s uncached; worktree clean before and after the mutation; head zero behind main.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f19348629bdab91c8d5ab8966e2f5b281e9e0d86","head":"fe9f25a789397cd1154739b10bfc7b60599ca2be","verdict":"approved"}}